        {
            "id": "7676054",
            "user": "Mkl654321",
            "timestamp": "17:19, 1 May 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(3,866 bytes)",
            "change": -1229,
            "summary": "(Made intro more concise as readers would already be well aware of what shrink tubing is and does.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "{{fa}}[[Image:JLS_3rd_Sleeving2_800.jpg|300px|right]]This article will show you how to expand electrical-insulating heat shrink tubing by stretching it.",
                    "#Select the right tool for the size of the heat shrink tubing you want to expand.<br><br> Most smaller tubing is easy to stretch with needle-nose pliers. If possible, find a pair with extended ends to accommodate longer sections of tubing.<br><br>[[Image:JLS_Retro_PC_sleeving_small.jpg|center|300px|500px|Needlenose pliers.]]",
                    "# To stretch larger tubing, use a combination of two pliers to expand the tubing as shown in this illustration.<br><br>[[Image:JLS_Retro_PC_sleeving_large.jpg|center|300px|500px|Large pliers in combination with regular pliers.]]",
                    "#*Extremely small tubing calls for tweezers with needlepoint tips, such as LTD's Tweezerman models.<br><br>[[Image:JLS_Retro_PC_sleeving_mini.jpg|center|300px|500px|Needlepoint tweezers with a jeweler's screwdriver.]]",
                    "#Stretch the tubing only a little at a time to avoid tearing or puncturing it.",
                    "#Repeat steps 2 through 5 until you have rotated the tubing enough to return to your starting point.",
                    "#Flip the tubing to so that the other end of it fits over the tweezers or pliers, and repeat the above steps.",
                    "#The tubing is now ready for use.",
                    "*Good-quality tubing can expand to more than twice its size. Stretching as described above should not puncture or tear it.",
                    "*Strectched/expanded tubing will shrink much faster, using less heat, than an equivalent piece of tubing that has not been stretched.",
                    "*Use the following for determining safe dielectric withstanding voltages when expanding heat shrink tubing: For '''every mil''' (1/1000 of an inch) in thickness, typical Polyolefin heat shrink tubing (MIL-DTL-23053/5, Class 1 and 3 specification), with a standard wall thickness tolerance of ± 20%, will have a dielectric withstanding voltage of '''500 volts'''. The smallest available heat shrink tubing has a thickness of 0.2 mil, even though most sizes are around 1 mil. For the smallest heat shrink tubing, expanding it to double its size may reduce its thickness by up to 50%, which is 0.1 mil. Multiple this by its withstanding voltage of 500V/mil, and the smallest heat-shrink tubing will still withstand 50V. However, when the tubing is heat-shrunk, its thickness will increase, which will in turn increase its overall dielectric withstanding voltage.",
                    "*Be careful when cutting off sections of the sleeving. If the cut is uneven or jagged, that will make the tubing more susceptible to tearing when stretched.",
                    "*Be gentle with delicate tweezers. Using any metal object to pry them apart, as in the illustrations above, can damage them."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "{{fa}}[[Image:JLS_3rd_Sleeving2_800.jpg|300px|right]]Applying heat shrink tubing to an [[Do Common Electrical Repairs|electrical connection]] is an effective means of adding electrical protection and physical stability. It helps isolate electrical contacts, connectors, and exposed wire to reduce the risk of accidental shorts. It is a handy consumable to have when working with electronics and [[Solder Stereo Mini Plugs|soldering]], but what if you don't have the right size?<br><br>This simple guide will help you learn how to expand smaller tubing to fit over larger electrical components and wiring while using [[Remove Rust Spots From Tools|everyday tools]].",
                    "",
                    "#Select the right tool for the size of the heat shrink tubing you want to expand.<br><br>",
                    "#*Smaller and moderate sized heat shrink tubing is easy to stretch with needle-nose pliers. Most tubing sold is of this size. If possible, find a pair of needle-nose pliers with extended ends to accommodate longer sections of tubing.<br><br>[[Image:JLS_Retro_PC_sleeving_small.jpg|center|300px|500px|Needlenose pliers.]]",
                    "#*Larger tubing will require larger pliers. Some tubing will benefit from the combination of pliers to leverage enough force to stretch thicker tubing. In this scenario, one plier's handles will expand the tubing, while the other pliers are used to pry them apart.<br><br>[[Image:JLS_Retro_PC_sleeving_large.jpg|center|300px|500px|Large pliers in combination with regular pliers.]]",
                    "#*Extremely small tubing calls for tweezers with needlepoint tips. A good example is LTD's Tweezerman models.<br><br>[[Image:JLS_Retro_PC_sleeving_mini.jpg|center|300px|500px|Needlepoint tweezers with a jeweler's screwdriver.]]",
                    "#Stretch the tubing only a little at a time. Avoid tearing or puncturing it which could be the result of expanding it too fast or expanding it with too much pressure in one spot.",
                    "#Repeat steps 2 through 5 until you rotate the tubing enough to return to your original stretching point.",
                    "#Flip the tubing to so that the other end of it fits over the tweezers or pliers and repeat.",
                    "#Use the tubing by slipping it over your wire or other electrical component and heat-activating it as you normally would.",
                    "*Most tubing can expand more than twice its size. This is a good way to gauge the quality of the tubing you have. If it breaks before expanding to twice its size, the tubing may be of lower quality than most others.",
                    "*If you're puncturing your tubing or stretching it too much at the ends of your pliers (aka: pox-marking), try a different tool or a longer set of pliers.",
                    "*Expanded tubing will shrink much faster with less heat than at its original size.",
                    "*Guide for determining safe dielectric withstanding voltages when expanding heat shrink tubing: For '''every mil''' (1/1000 of an inch) in thickness, typical Polyolefin heat shrink tubing (MIL-DTL-23053/5, Class 1 and 3 specification), with a standard wall thickness tolerance of ± 20%, will have a dielectric withstanding voltage of '''500 volts'''. The smallest available heat shrink tubing has a thickness of 0.2 mil, even though most sizes are around 1 mil. For the smallest heat shrink tubing, expanding it to double its size may reduce its thickness by up to 50% (although tests show its the same thickness after expanding), which is 0.1 mil. Multiple this by its withstanding of 500V/mil, and the smallest heat-shrink tubing will still withstand 50V. However, when the tubing is shrunk again, its thickness increases, which increases its overall dielectric withstanding voltage.",
                    "*Be careful when cutting off sections of the sleeving. If the cut is uneven or jagged, it makes the tubing more susceptible to tearing when stretched. Expand only sleeving that has been evenly cut at each end.",
                    "*Be gentle with delicate tweezers. Using any metal object to pry them apart, such as jeweler's screwdrivers, can leave marks on the tweezers."
                ]
            }
        },
